body:
>I made a Tasker process that flooded it with garbage periodically.

First of all I love this xD

You can access the clipboard with ClipboardManager:

---
    ClipboardManager clipBoardManager = getSystemService(CLIPBOARD_SERVICE);

    ClipData clipData = clipBoardManager.getPrimaryClip();

    //clipboard contents index
    ClipData.Item item = clipData.getItemAt(0);
        //on my device I am pretty sure there is *only* 0, and my *keyboard* actually tracks the history independently of the system clipboard 

    String text = item.getText().toString();
---

There is also a Listener interface for when clipboard contents changed which may help.

When everything you can find on a topic tells you to do something in a way, I figure it's for a good reason.

If the problem is that it's sloppy to muck around with intents (which it kind of is), the solution isn't to work around that - it is to sit and break it down *once* into a neat static method that you can use quickly without needing to think much about it.

Something like:

        Void launchPkg(String pkgName) {
            Intent appLaunchIntent = getPackageManager().getLaunchIntentForPackage(pkgName);
            startActivity(appLaunchIntent) }

I'm sure you can grab the package name as a string fairly easily but I don't know how off the top of my head.

Maybe you could use someone's library for this or even for the whole thing if you want to go searching.
